In article <gZfICau00WB8IUkWJa@andrew.cmu.edu> dh07+@andrew.cmu.edu (David Hairston) writes:

[In an earlier article I wrote:]
>>Why do I think this is a great idea? Because I'm getting bored with having
>>all the levels looking about the same i.e. same sized rectangles.
>..
>neato!  how's this:  have ladders exist in run-of-the-mill rooms of
>normal dungeon levels.  the ladders would lead to single-room attics
>(or cellars).  the attic could itself have an additional ladder
>leading to another attic or perhaps to a cellar (i.e.  you go up
>ladder A and down ladder B and you're not back on the original level.)
>the attics would not be shown as being a part of the previous dungeon
>level.  golems are known to frequent these rooms and a rope golem is
>always to be found there.  on occasion, the rooms are over-populated
>with partying monsters who are more than glad to have you join them
>(for dinner).  attics are always dark.
Hmm.. Ideas come better in the making. Some observations:
 - Cellars are also found on some shops. The shopkeepers use them to
   store spare supplies.
 - The castle has a tower on its every corner.
 - Perhaps you could find a shorcut between levels 7 and 22, say. This
   would make climb through several attics/cellars. You could implement
   the Endless Stair of Moria. (Khazad-dum, not the game.)
 - Some ladders would have a lockable hatch.
 - Me thinks that no other game has attics/cellars like these. A chance
   to make Nethack more original. Am I mistaken?
 - If you dig yourself out of the single room of tower, you fall..
 - You can't dig yourself out of cellars or attics.
 - Cellars are moist. Even being there for a longer period might rust
   your sword.

Speaking of moist another (Oh no, not another ye shriek! ;) idea came
to me. Why not make underground rivers? Just put enough pools in a level
for them to reach from one end of the level to the other, in diagonal
manner. To get over one you might need boots of jumping (what are they called?)
or a wand of cold; making a very temporary ice bridge. Wands of fire wouldn't
work because the flowing water quickly fills the evaporated squares of water.
After that you could add any number of additional things. I leave this to
your imagination. I just mention a boat.

